## Robot Movement and Mathematical Representation

The robotic arm is a **5R serial manipulator**, meaning that all five joints are revolute joints and each joint contributes one rotational degree of freedom. The motion of the robot is therefore fundamentally described in **joint space** by the vector

$$
\mathbf{q}
==========

\begin{bmatrix}
q_1 \
q_2 \
q_3 \
q_4 \
q_5
\end{bmatrix}
$$

where each (q_i) represents the rotation of the corresponding revolute joint. In the present kinematic model, the five joints rotate about the (Z), (Y), (Y), (-Y), and (X) axes respectively. The first joint provides rotation about the base (Z)-axis, while the next joints provide the shoulder, elbow, and wrist-related rotations. Consequently, the robot does not move as a single rigid body or along one fixed plane; its overall movement is produced by the combined rotation of all five joint variables.

The motion of each revolute joint is represented mathematically by a **screw axis**. For a revolute joint with axis direction (\boldsymbol{\omega}) and a point (\mathbf{q}) on that axis, the corresponding screw axis is

$$
\mathcal{S}
===========

\begin{bmatrix}
\boldsymbol{\omega} \
\mathbf{v}
\end{bmatrix}
$$

with

$$
\mathbf{v}
==========

-\boldsymbol{\omega}\times\mathbf{q}
$$

This representation describes the instantaneous spatial motion associated with each joint. The five screw axes obtained for the robot are therefore the mathematical representation of its five independent joint motions.

The complete joint motion can be represented using the joint-variable vector (\mathbf{q}), while the instantaneous spatial motion of the end effector can be related to the joint velocities through the robot Jacobian:

$$
\mathbf{V}
==========

\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})\dot{\mathbf{q}}
$$

where (\dot{\mathbf{q}}) is the vector of joint angular velocities and (\mathbf{V}) is the spatial velocity of the end effector. Thus, a change in any individual joint angle affects the final position and/or orientation of the end effector through the combined kinematics of the serial chain.

The physical geometry documented for the robot places the main arm dimensions along the (Z)-direction, with additional offsets along (Y) and a wrist displacement along (X). The coordinate positions used in the screw-axis derivation are (q_1=(0,0,0)), (q_2=(0,2.5,5)), (q_3=(0,3.5,21.5)), (q_4=(0,2.5,39)), and (q_5=(1.5,0,39)), all in centimetres. These coordinates define the spatial arrangement of the joint axes and therefore determine the geometric workspace in which the end effector can move.

The robot's movement can therefore be understood as a **continuous trajectory in configuration space**. As the sensed human-arm motion changes, the corresponding robotic joint variables can change with time,

$$
\mathbf{q}=\mathbf{q}(t)
$$

which produces a time-varying robot configuration. The end-effector pose is consequently a function of these joint variables,

$$
\mathbf{T}(t)=f\big(\mathbf{q}(t)\big)
$$

and its trajectory in physical space is generated by the combined rotational motion of the five joints. The resulting path is therefore not assumed to be a predefined straight line, circle, or other geometric curve. It is generated from the changing joint configuration commanded by the human-to-robot interface.

In this system, the wearable sensing stage provides the input from the operator's arm. The repository identifies the use of the **MPU6050, flex sensors, and strain gauges** to capture motion and activity, with the processed sensor information used to control the robotic system. The intended mathematical chain is therefore:

$$
\text{Human Motion}
\rightarrow
\text{Sensor Measurements}
\rightarrow
\text{Processed Motion Information}
\rightarrow
\mathbf{q}(t)
\rightarrow
\mathbf{T}(t)
\rightarrow
\text{3D Robot Trajectory}
$$

This means that the robot's spatial path is an outcome of the **time-varying joint configuration**, rather than an independently specified path. The five joint variables describe the robot in configuration space, while the forward kinematic mapping describes where the end effector is located and how it is oriented in physical three-dimensional space.

